+# Permission at tool dispatch
+
+The existing PermissionService, Project rules writer and permission broker remain the only
+permission authorities. The executor retains whether this exact call received an explicit
+approval; this transient fact is neither a new grant store nor a Project rule.
+The binding captures the exact normalized input bytes, while the final synchronous registration
+and handler checks retain tool identity. Existing trusted hook and tool-specific approval gates
+keep their call-scoped authorization; ordinary automatic rule allowances do not acquire one.
+
+```text
+initial policy → optional broker approval → started event → current-policy recheck → handler
+                                                     └→ changed Ask / Deny: refuse dispatch
+```
+
+A Project rule changed from automatic Allow to Ask before dispatch cannot inherit the old
+automatic allowance. Refuse that attempt with a reason requiring a fresh approval; a resubmitted
+call uses the ordinary broker. An unchanged Ask already approved for this call must not ask twice.
+Current Deny, a revoked registration, changed handler and cancellation still prevent execution.
+
+Desktop continuous delivery and remote replay observe the same existing permission/tool events.
+No protocol, storage schema, mode policy, approval UI or persisted grant is migrated.
+
+Acceptance uses AgentRuntime.executeTurn with controlled model replies, the real Project/SQLite
+writer and a real local effect: Allow→Ask, Allow→Deny, approved Ask→Ask, and a subsequent fresh
+request through the original broker. Rules change during the awaited started-event publication,
+before the actual effect, rather than replacing the permission checker with a mock.
